

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Locked: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, behind a single module so the storage choice stays swappable.

  Last question before I put approaches in front of you. Two clearing rules are in regardless — overwrite when a
  [3mdifferent[0m user logs in, and expose a [38;5;153mclear()[39m for a future logout — so this is only about the case nobody triggers
  either one.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a stored id on a shared or borrowed browser outlives the person who created it. Expiry is the only thing
  that bounds that, and it's also what your retention story rests on if this ever becomes personal data.

  - [1m30-day TTL[0m — a returning user stays recognized across a normal absence; a borrowed laptop forgets within a month.
    Standard retention window, easy to defend.
  - [1m7-day TTL[0m — much tighter exposure on shared machines, at the cost of frequently treating genuine returning users as
    unknown.
  - [1mNo expiry[0m — the id persists until something explicitly clears it. Simplest code, and the strongest recognition. But
    on a shared browser it's indefinite, and "we keep it forever" is the hardest posture to justify if a retention
    question ever lands on you.

  [1mMy recommendation: 30 days.[0m It's the conventional window, it bounds shared-device exposure without making recognition
  useless, and the number is a constant in one module — genuinely cheap to change later, unlike the two decisions above
  it.
